  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The bin card is a quantity record; the stores ledger prices every receipt and issue.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The requisition comes from the department that needs the material and is the authority to issue.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    200 × 10 = 2,000 plus 50 × 12 = 600, total ₦2,600.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    LIFO issues the newest units first: the 250 units all come from the 300 received at ₦12, so 250 × 12 = ₦3,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A new average is calculated at every receipt and used until the next one.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    LIFO issues the dearest units first, leaving the cheapest in stock.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The EOQ balances the cost of placing orders against the cost of holding stock.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reorder level = maximum usage × maximum lead time = 500 × 4 = 2,000 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2,000 − (400 × 3) = 800 units.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The records show what should be there and the count shows what is; the gap is a loss.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Time rate pays for attendance; piece rate pays for output.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The wage is the number of units produced multiplied by the rate per unit.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The worker is present and paid, but a breakdown or a shortage means no output is produced.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It cannot be traced to a product, so it is carried as an indirect factory cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Basic 40 × 400 = ₦16,000; overtime 6 × 600 = ₦3,600; gross pay ₦19,600.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Basic rate × hours is direct labour; the extra half is the premium.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The order caused the cost, so it carries it.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The employer collects it and pays it over; it is not an expense of the business.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is paid by the employer in addition to wages, so it is a labour cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The clock card records attendance; the job card or time book allocates the hours to jobs.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    500,000 ÷ 25,000 = ₦20 per labour hour.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Absorbed = 24,000 × 20 = 480,000; actual 470,000, so 10,000 more was absorbed than spent.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Absorbed is less than actual, so the profit and loss account is debited with the difference.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The firm charged too much to its products, so the correction is a credit.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cost belongs wholly to one department, so it is allocated rather than shared.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The cost is common to several departments and is divided on a fair basis.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The canteen serves people, so the number of employees is the fair basis.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Maintenance is driven by the use of the machines.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The basis must reflect how the overhead is incurred, and hours or wages are the usual measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It measures how many times the stock is sold and replaced in a period.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stock is being sold and replaced quickly, which ties up less capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They are the costs of keeping stock, and they rise with the order size.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A larger order means more stock held, so holding cost rises while ordering cost falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It measures the proportion of the workforce that has to be replaced in a period.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each replacement has to be found, trained and brought up to speed.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Net pay is what the worker actually receives after PAYE, pension and union dues.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The employer also bears pension contributions and other on-costs, which are not part of gross pay.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    FIFO leaves the most recent receipts in stock, so the closing stock uses the newer price.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A running record is kept for every item, and it is checked against a physical count.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Holding cost is the cost of keeping the stock: storage, insurance and the capital tied up in it.
  ]

]

